+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Strengths:** Combines the geometric fidelity of 3D grids with the memory efficiency of triplanes [S1]. Handles aliasing via VR-NeRF methods [S1]. Uses spatially adaptive surfaceness for better surface reconstruction [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Negatively impacts quality near fine objects (lamp wires) and transparent structures (glass door) if not carefully tuned [S1]. Requires careful tuning of the surfaceness parameter $\beta(\mathbf{x})$ [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When high-quality geometry and rendering are required, and memory efficiency is a concern compared to pure 3D grid methods [S1].
+*   **When not:** When rendering very fine details or transparent structures, as quality may degrade [S1].
 
 ## Background
-HybridNeRF was introduced in the paper "HybridNeRF: Efficient Neural Rendering via Adaptive Volumetric Surfaces" [S1]. It builds upon the concept of hybrid representations that combine explicit grids with implicit MLPs, aiming to balance the efficiency of explicit features with the flexibility of implicit networks. The SDF formulation and surfaceness field were introduced to improve surface reconstruction quality [S1]. The FastSR-NeRF pipeline [S2] later demonstrated the effectiveness of combining this architecture with super-resolution for consumer device efficiency.
+HybridNeRF was introduced in the paper "HybridNeRF: Efficient Neural Rendering via Adaptive Volumetric Surfaces" [S1]. It builds on previous work in neural radiance fields, specifically combining ideas from multi-resolution 3D feature grids and triplane-based representations [S1]. The method uses a proposal network similar to Nerfacto to improve sampling efficiency [S1].
 
